1c:["$","$L32",null,{"type":"multiple","children":[["$","$L33",null,{"title":"Can I get hermeticity without Nix?","id":"can-i-get-hermeticity-without-nix","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":["You can get ",["$","em",null,{"children":"reproducibility across a fleet"}]," by baking the toolchain into\none worker image and routing every action to it. That's path 1 at the top\nof this page, and for many teams it's enough. What you don't get is matching\naction digests between a developer's laptop and the fleet, because the\nlaptop isn't running that image. Nix pinning is what closes that gap."]}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Note this is a client-side requirement only. The Forge server, the\nscheduler, and the workers run fine with no Nix anywhere."}]]}],["$","$L33",null,{"title":"How is this different from local testing?","id":"how-is-this-different-from-local-testing","children":["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/remote-execution/local-testing","children":"Local testing"}]," validates the ",["$","em",null,{"children":"protocol"}],"\nwith whatever toolchain is on your ",["$","code",null,{"children":"PATH"}],"; it deliberately has no toolchain\nopinion, which is what makes it a good first step. This page pins the\ntoolchain so local and remote actions hash identically. Do that one first if\nyou just want to see ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Execute"}]," work."]}]}],["$","$L33",null,{"title":"Does hermeticity slow my build down?","id":"does-hermeticity-slow-my-build-down","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The first ",["$","code",null,{"children":"nix develop"}]," is slow; it's fetching a full toolchain closure,\nand 15 to 20 GB of free disk is a reasonable expectation. After that it's a\nno-op. At build time, pinned toolchains are marginally ",["$","em",null,{"children":"faster"}]," to cache\nagainst, because the digest stability means fewer spurious misses."]}]}],["$","$L33",null,{"title":"Which family should I use for a mixed-language repo?","id":"which-family-for-a-mixed-language-repo","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"They compose: a workspace can register Rust, C++, and Java toolchains at\nonce, and Bazel resolves per target. The practical constraint is that only\nRust has native non-Linux configs, so on macOS your C++ and Java actions\nneed a reachable worker while your Rust actions can still run locally."}]}]]}]
